Hello, Thank you for contacting the Immigration Representatives Mailbox. Question: If an employee is working 40 hours on a LMIA exempt job under R 205 (D) under NOC 4154 and the employer decides to reduce the hours from 40 hours to 30 hours, what should the employer do? Answer: Guidance regarding hours of work is provided on the IRCC website (Employer Compliance Inspections). See the section titled "Working conditions / acceptable changes". Working conditions Working conditions may include those non-wage related remuneration benefits and entitlements specifically detailed in the Offer of Employment, such as
- location of employment;
- statutory holidays, sick and vacation days;
- hours of work (including overtime);
- transportation costs (where applicable);
- accommodations, conditions and costs (where applicable);
- health or medical insurance; and/or
- other non-taxable benefits.
Note: Many other working conditions and workplace standards (e.g., obligations around dismissals and rights to file
complaints, occupational health and safety regulations and recruitment laws), are governed by provincial and territorial legislation. Employers of foreign workers must also comply with these laws in order to meet IMP requirements. Acceptable changes Employers are not required to proactively inform IRCC of changes in working conditions. However, they will have to provide evidence of an acceptable justification [R203(1.1)] should a random inspection be initiated and if there are differences between the working conditions stated in the offer of employment and those at the time of inspection.
In such cases, when requested during an inspection, the employer should submit documentation to substantiate their claim that the reduction of working hours is justified (e.g., evidence of the business's finances). The employer must keep all documents related to any changes from the original Offer of Employment submitted to IRCC for a period of six years following the issuance date of the foreign worker's work permit. Additionally, employers who have concerns that they are not meeting conditions of the Offer of Employment can use the Voluntary Disclosure option. Regards, The Immigration Representatives Mailbox

