At the time of application, the visa office was correct. Permanent Resident applications that were being processed outside of Canada were not eligible for the In-Canada exemption, because they were not eligible in-Canada applications.
Fortunately in the interim, the In-Canada policy has been amended to better facilitate clients in our TR to PR pathways.
The public policy now applies to a foreign national who applied on or after July 31, 2018 to remain in Canada as a permanent resident as a member of one of the following classes:
Federal Skilled Worker Class (pursuant to subsection 75(1) of the IRPR);
Transitional Federal Skilled Worker Class (pursuant to subsection 85.1(1) of the IRPR);
Quebec Skilled Worker Class (pursuant to subsection 86(1) of the IRPR);
Provincial Nominee Class (pursuant to subsection 87(1) of the IRPR);
Canadian Experience Class (pursuant to subsection 87.1(1) of the IRPR);
Federal Skilled Trades Class (pursuant to subsection 87.2(1) of the IRPR); or
Live-in Caregiver Class (pursuant to section 110 of the IRPR).
This would include your client and make them eligible for the In-Canada exemption.
Please note that in-Canada biometric service will be available at 58 Service Canada locations across the country as of December 2019. The In-Canada public policy exemptions will no longer apply after the in-Canada service is introduced and clients in these situations will no longer be biometrically-exempt.
We hope this has answered your question. Please do not hesitate to follow-up if you require any further clarification.
Regards,
The Immigration Representatives Mailbox

